WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.790
Our next guest is a two-time GRAMMY award winning

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.310
singer and songwriter, and now she's taking pen to paper

3
00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:11.420
with her very first children's book, "The Curly Girl Blues".

4
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.432
Everyone, Please welcome Tori Kelly!

5
00:00:14.432 --> 00:00:17.849
(audience cheering)
Hi!

6
00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:21.660
Speaking of GRAMMYs,

7
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.830
you're nominated for two GRAMMYs?

8
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.510
Yes. It's a huge, huge honor.

9
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.600 line:15%
I mean, I remember being nominated

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.780 line:15%
for the first time for "Best New Artist",

11
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.990 line:15%
like when I was new artist.
Yeah.

12
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.580
And just starting out, and it was this whole

13
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.610
like whirlwind of emotions, and that just trips me out.

14
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.140
It was my childhood dream.

15
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.350
So the fact that I have those is just surreal to me.

16
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.840
But, where did your GRAMMYs end up?

17
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.000
So, what people don't know

18
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.730
is you go on stage and you accept the award and everything,

19
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.530
and people think that's your actual GRAMMY,

20
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.270
like, you go home with that, and that's not true at all.

21
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.390
It's just a prop.

22
00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.320
So everyone's just holding the same GRAMMY.

23
00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.909
And they send you-
And they like

24
00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:08.210
Cinderella it right out of your hands.

25
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.300
Yeah, so you walk off stage,

26
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.133
and you're like, "Oh my gosh",

27
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:11.320
and they're like, "Thank you so much",

28
00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.584
and they just take it away.
(audience laughing)

29
00:01:13.584 --> 00:01:14.424
Like basically-
Right, like what

30
00:01:14.424 --> 00:01:15.650
are you gonna do at that moment?

31
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.460
Yeah, they send you the GRAMMYs

32
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.120
in the mail, like way later.

33
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.210
So this was like months and months and months later,

34
00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.860
and I finally get a notification.

35
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.510
So they were like, "Hey, you weren't home.

36
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:33.160
We tried to deliver this package and you weren't here,

37
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.030
so we sent them to the local drug store."

38
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.180
You know, where you can pick up just packages,

39
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.560
and we filmed the whole thing,

40
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.127
'cause we thought it was so funny, and being like,

41
00:01:41.127 --> 00:01:46.127
"Hey, I think there's two packages here, like this big."

42
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.840
They're gold.
Yeah, and golden.

43
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.898
Okay. So you are Meena in "Sing".

44
00:01:52.898 --> 00:01:55.360
(audience cheering)

45
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.500
Meena is such a meaningful

46
00:01:58.898 --> 00:02:03.340
and important character in both movies,

47
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.790
and one of Meena's storylines in "Sing 2",

48
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.470
is that she is with Eric Andre, Darius,

49
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.390
who's like totally full of himself and hilarious,

50
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.540
but she's not attracted to him,

51
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.160
and they have to pretend like they're in love.

52
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.790
So what happens?

53
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.780
Pharrell is another one of our amazing co-stars,

54
00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.760
and he plays an elephant named Alfonzo,

55
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.850
and that is who Meena actually has a crush on,

56
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.330
and it's so cute, and so, you know

57
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.540
by the end, you're seeing Meena kind of picturing

58
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.570
that it's him instead of Darius,

59
00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.560
and that kinda helps her get through the performance,

60
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:42.580 line:15%
'cause they have a duet together,

61
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.590 line:15%
and she's like "How am I supposed to pretend

62
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:45.423 line:15%
that I like this guy?"

63
00:02:45.423 --> 00:02:49.290 line:15%
So it's really sweet. I love her storyline in this movie.

64
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.790
I really also love the fact that you did not,

65
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.510
with the GRAMMYs and the starring roles

66
00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.393
in the big franchise movies,

67
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.820
that is not exactly where your story started,

68
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.830
and for me, I always like to laugh,

69
00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.520
and I love the lighter side of life,

70
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.070
but I like the depth too.

71
00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.170
What was the struggle? What was the journey?

72
00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.703
It wasn't always easy for you.

73
00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:14.803
It wasn't, no.

74
00:03:16.742 --> 00:03:19.620
Basically, I went into record labels

75
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:20.920
when I was like 12 years old.

76
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.400
I did a ton of different competition TV shows,

77
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.170
and got a lot of rejection along the way.

78
00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.260
Nothing was panning out,

79
00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.947
and finally I was like,

80
00:03:30.947 --> 00:03:33.640
"You know what? I've gotten all of these no's,

81
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.570
I'm gonna just kind of take my career into my own hands",

82
00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.670
and that's when I started just posting videos online,

83
00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:44.010
on YouTube, and just doing covers with friends,

84
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.500
and picking up the guitar,

85
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.130
and I was just having so much fun with it.

86
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.880
I didn't care anymore, like who was watching,

87
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.970
and slowly but surely, people started noticing,

88
00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.619
and I started getting to do shows.

89
00:03:55.619 --> 00:03:58.849
(audience cheering)

90
00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:00.430
It's been amazing.

91
00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.140
I mean, I think without all of that rejection

92
00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.960
at such a young age, maybe I wouldn't have

93
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.980
had that same drive and that same fire

94
00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.760
to be able to do what I'm doing now, even.

95
00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.483
And your Curly Girl Blues inspired

96
00:04:17.483 --> 00:04:20.700
from your own childhood.
Yes.

97
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:21.930
Tell me about that.

98
00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.660
Yeah, so I have curly hair,

99
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.430
and I did not like it growing up.

100
00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.263
At school and stuff,

101
00:04:29.263 --> 00:04:30.990 line:15%
I didn't really see my hair,

102
00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.090 line:15%
and I was very insecure about it,

103
00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.470 line:15%
and I wanted to write a book

104
00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.870 line:15%
that could hopefully inspire other kids.

105
00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.760
If I went through this, and I didn't like my hair,

106
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:42.910
and was struggling with it,

107
00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.502
there's gotta be other kids who also-

108
00:04:45.502 --> 00:04:47.325
(audience applauding)

109
00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:50.380
Could feel the same way.
Well, once again,

110
00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.560
you took your discomfort,
Mm-hmm.

111
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.010
and turned it into your empowerment,

112
00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.700
and those messages resonate with people.

113
00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.760
They resonate with me so much.
Mm-hmm.

114
00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.570
As a last question, may I ask you,

115
00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.870
is it true that you were pen pals with your husband?

116
00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.370
We were, yeah, it was very,

117
00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.090
it just wasn't flirty at all in the beginning.

118
00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.030
We were just writing to each other

119
00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.540
on different sides of the world,

120
00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.410
and then for like five months we were doing that,

121
00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.347
and we finally met, and it was like,

122
00:05:17.347 --> 00:05:19.225
"Wow, we like each other!"

123
00:05:19.225 --> 00:05:20.300
(audience applauding)

124
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.133
Long story short.

125
00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:24.433
Well, I think that goes to show us all

126
00:05:24.433 --> 00:05:26.259
keep writing everybody.

127
00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:28.027
(audience cheering)
It's such an amazing

128
00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:32.857
way to communicate, as is writing a children's book.

129
00:05:32.857 --> 00:05:35.700
Go to thecurlygirlblues.com to get

130
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.213
your amazing copy of this amazing book.

131
00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:41.880 line:15%
(bright music)

